<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wei River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Wei River is the main tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a>, flowing east through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Its valley, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanzhong">Guanzhong</a> plain, was the homeland of the Zhou and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> and the site of the capitals Hao, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianyang">Xianyang</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>. Turkic armies reached it in 626 to threaten the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wei-river.jpg" alt="Wei River" width="320" height="264" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kmusser · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Weirivermap.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Wei River is 818 km long and drains an area of 135,000 square kilometres. It is the largest tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a>. It rises in the mountains of southern Weiyuan County in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a>, less than 110 km from the Yellow River at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lanzhou">Lanzhou</a>. Because the Yellow River turns sharply north there to form the Ordos Loop, the two rivers do not meet for more than 2,000 km further along the Yellow River's course.</p><p>The Wei runs east past <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tianshui">Tianshui</a>, enters Mount Long and emerges as the principal river of the Guanzhong Basin. It passes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baoji">Baoji</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianyang">Xianyang</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xian">Xi'an</a> and Weinan and joins the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> at Tongguan County, near where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanxi">Shanxi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a> meet. Its tributaries include the Luo, Jing, Niutou, Feng and Chishui. The valley has a continental climate, with hot summers and cool, dry winters. Its natural steppe-forest vegetation has been transformed by human activity, and it is mostly agricultural.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In Chinese myth the giant Kua Fu drained the Yellow and Wei rivers to quench his thirst as he pursued the Sun. The valley was one of the early cradles of Chinese civilisation. The capitals of the Zhou, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a>, Han and Tang dynasties were situated along it, and the area of Dingxi near its headwaters has numerous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-age">Stone Age</a> sites. The valley is likely the earliest centre of Chinese civilisation and the site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s first major irrigation works. Some Chinese historians believe the Wei is the ancient Jiang River that gave its name to the families of Shennong and the Yan emperor, credited with early agriculture there.</p><p>The headwaters served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s natural gateway to the west. The Northern Silk Road ran from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baoji">Baoji</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tianshui">Tianshui</a>, then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lanzhou">Lanzhou</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang">Dunhuang</a>, and looped north of the Taklamakan on its way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashgar">Kashgar</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/1556-shaanxi-earthquake">1556 Shaanxi earthquake</a> had its epicentre in the valley.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Sanmenxia Dam, built in 1960, caused extensive sedimentation in the lower reaches. The Baojixia diversion works of 1972 created an irrigation district of 128,000 hectares, which reduced flows. Heavy rain in September 2003 caused flooding that killed over 30 people and displaced over 300,000. A train bridge over the Wei appeared on the 5000-yuan note of the first series of the renminbi, dated 1953.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wei River” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Zhou, a people of the <b>Wei River</b> valley who had been Shang allies and subordinates, grew powerful under King Wen and built alliances with other western peoples.</blockquote><a href="/empire/shang-dynasty">Shang dynasty · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Zhou were a people of the <b>Wei River</b> valley in modern Shaanxi.</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-zhou">Western Zhou · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the <b>Wei River</b> valley the Kexingzhuang II sites already show a decline that scholars put down to migration caused by environmental change.</blockquote><a href="/culture/longshan-culture">Longshan culture · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>An earthquake centred in the <b>Wei River</b> valley killed an estimated 830,000 people.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-23">23 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>During the collapse of the Sui, Shibi Khagan besieged Emperor Yang at Yanmen in 615, and his successors backed Chinese rebels and threatened the new Tang dynasty, reaching the <b>Wei River</b> near Chang'an in 626.</blockquote><a href="/state/eastern-turkic-khaganate">Eastern Turkic Khaganate</a></li><li><blockquote>Western Guo was founded by a brother of King Wen of Zhou near Baoji in the <b>Wei River</b> valley.</blockquote><a href="/state/western-guo">Western Guo</a></li></ul></section>
